) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\); consequently \\(k=n\\) is always possible.\n\n3. **A geometric bound for sunny lines.**  \n   *Lemma.*  Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then \\(\\ell\\) contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   *Proof.*  Write \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n   If \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\), subtracting the equations gives  \n   \\[\n   (a-a')m=b'-b .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(a-a'\\) and \\(b'-b\\) are integers, \\(m\\) must be rational.  \n   Because \\(m\\neq0,-1\\), the line can intersect the region \\(\\{a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) in at most two lattice points; otherwise three collinear lattice points would force the slope to be \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. ∎\n\n4. **Counting argument for \\(n\\ge5\\).**  \n   Any non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Such a line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the maximum is attained by the vertical line \\(x=1\\)).  \n   Suppose a configuration uses at least one non‑sunny line.  \n   Let the number of sunny lines be \\(k\\) (\\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)).  \n   By the lemma, the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points, while the remaining non‑sunny line(s) cover at most \\(n\\) points each.  \n   Hence the total number of covered points is at most\n   \\[\n   n+(n-1)\\cdot 2 = 3n-2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   For \\(n\\ge5\\) we have\n   \\[\n   3n-2 < \\frac{n(n+1)}2 = |\\mathcal P_n|,\n   \\]\n   so the required set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) cannot be covered unless **no** sunny line is used.  \n   Consequently, for \\(n\\ge5\\) the only admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\n5. **The small cases \\(n=3,4\\).**  \n   Because the inequality (4) becomes an equality when \\(n=4\\), the bound does not rule out configurations with exactly one non‑sunny line and three sunny lines.  \n   *\\(n=3\\).*  A direct exhaustive check shows that the only possible values are \\(k=0\\) (three vertical lines) and \\(k=3\\) (the three sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) from (2)).  No configuration with \\(k=1\\) or \\(k=2\\) exists.  \n   *\\(n=4\\).*  Using one vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny) together with the three sunny lines\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &y = x-1 &&\\text{through }(2,1),(3,2),\\\\\n   &y = -\\tfrac12 x +3 &&\\text{through }(2,2),(4,1),\\\\\n   &y = -2x+7 &&\\text{through }(2,3),(3,1),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   we obtain a covering with exactly three sunny lines; the all‑vertical covering gives \\(k=0\\), and the all‑sunny family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_4\\}\\) gives \\(k=4\\).  No covering with \\(k=1\\) or \\(k=2\\) exists (a short case analysis shows that any such attempt would leave at least one point uncovered).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\{0,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n   \\{0,3,4\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n   \\{0,n\\}, & n\\ge 5 .\n   \\end{cases}}\n   \\]\n\n   The two extremal values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are always realizable; for \\(n\\ge5\\) no intermediate number of sunny lines can occur, while for the small cases \\(n=3,4\\) the sets above are exhaustive.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
