overed is at most \\(4+4+1=9<10\\).  \n   If they belong to two different families, their intersection is a single lattice point, and again the total number of distinct points is at most \\(4+4+1=9\\).  \n   Consequently the ten points of \\(\\mathcal P_4\\) cannot be covered with exactly two sunny lines. ∎\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\) when \\(n\\ge5\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   Choose the first \\(n-k\\) columns, i.e. the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   These lines cover all points with abscissa \\(\\le n-k\\).  \n   The remaining points form the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal R=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a>n-k\\},\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   which consists of exactly \\(k\\) columns, the \\(j\\)-th column (counted from the left) containing the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k+j,\\,1),\\;(n-k+j,\\,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(n-k+j,\\;n+1-(n-k+j)\\bigr).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   For each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) pick a *different* point \\(P_j\\) from the already covered part \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n-k}V_i\\); for instance we may take  \n\n   \\[\n   P_j=(j,\\,1)\\in V_j .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Now define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\;\\text{the unique line through }P_j\\text{ and }(n-k+j,\\,1).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the two points have distinct abscissae, the slope of \\(S_j\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_j=\\frac{1-1}{\\,n-k+j-j\\,}=0\\quad\\text{if }j=1,\n   \\]\n\n   which would be non‑sunny; to avoid this we simply choose for \\(j=1\\) the point \\(P_1=(1,2)\\) instead of \\((1,1)\\).  With this slight modification every slope \\(m_j\\) is a rational number different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); consequently each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.  \n\n   The line \\(S_j\\) contains the point \\((n-k+j,1)\\) of the uncovered column and also the chosen point \\(P_j\\) that already belongs to a vertical line, so the two lines are distinct.  Moreover,