ig\\rfloor\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  In particular, for every \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{a sunny line contains at most }2\\text{ points of }\\mathcal P_n.}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines in a configuration.**  \n   Let a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n   A non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most\n   \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the longest column or row).  \n   Using (5) we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\,n+2k\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (6) for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge5\\) the right–hand side of (7) is \\(<4\\); consequently\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\le3\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge5.}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for every \\(n\\ge5\\).**  \n   Assume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n   By (5) the two sunny lines together cover at most four points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n   The remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-4\\) points must be covered by the\n   \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines.\n   Each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) new points, so the total number\n   of distinct points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   4+(n-2)n = n^2-2n+4 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge5\\),\n\n   \\[\n   n^2-2n+4 < \\frac{n(n+1)}2 ,\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the required number of points exceeds the maximal possible number.\n   Hence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for any\n   \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n5. **Existence of the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non‑sunny\n   and cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and one\n   sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)