}n\\ge4 } .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n5. **An upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\n   lines.  By (4) each sunny line contributes at most \\(s\\) points,\n   while each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points (a vertical,\n   a horizontal or a line \\(x+y=c\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\)\n   points).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,s+(n-k)\\,n\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (9) for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-s)} .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(s=\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) one checks that the right–hand side of\n   (10) never exceeds \\(3\\) for any \\(n\\ge3\\); indeed\n\n   * if \\(n=2m\\) (even) then \\(s=m\\) and (10) yields \\(k\\le 3\\);\n   * if \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd) then \\(s=m+1\\) and (10) again yields \\(k\\le 3\\).\n\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\le 3\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge3 } .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Putting the bounds together.**  \n   From (8) and (11) we obtain, for every \\(n\\ge4\\),\n\n   \\[\n   3\\le k\\le 3\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k=3 .\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n=3\\) the lower‑bound argument of § 4 does not apply (the set\n   \\(Q_3\\) is empty).  Direct inspection shows that \\(k=2\\) is impossible:\n   the six points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) are\n   \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\); any line that contains two\n   of them is non‑sunny, so with three lines at most one of them can be\n   sunny.  Hence for \\(n=3\\) the only possible values are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n7. **Explicit constructions for the admissible values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  The \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together\n   with the sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).\n\n   *\\(k=3\\) (all \\(n\\ge3\\)).*  \n   Use the two non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \n   The three remaining points \\((2,2),(2,3),(3,2)\\) are covered by the\n   three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   \\ell_2:\\;y=x-\\tfrac12,\\qquad\n   \\ell_3:\\;y=2x-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   All three have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and together with\n   \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) give a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines\n   (for \\(n=3\\) the two non‑sunny lines are omitted, leaving only the three\n   sunny lines).\n\n   Thus the values \\(k=0,1,3\\) are always attainable.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}}\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3 .\n   \\]\n\n   No configuration with \\(k=2\\) or with \\(k\\ge4\\) can exist, and the three\n   values displayed above are all realizable. ∎"
