e $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **How many points can a sunny line contain?**  \n   Write the equation of a sunny line in the reduced integer form  \n\n   \\[\n   p\\,x+q\\,y=r ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;p,q\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   All lattice points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on this line are  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,b)=(a_0+tq,\\;b_0-tp),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the parameter \\(t\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   |t|\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-(a_0+b_0)}{|p+q|}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a sunny line contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   1+2\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|p+q|}\\Big\\rfloor\\le\n   \\Big\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rceil\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Denote  \n\n   \\[\n   s:=\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Explicit constructions.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\); toge