er of sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines and let the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be\n   non‑sunny.  By the observation of step 3 each of the first \\(n-3\\) columns must\n   contain a vertical line; therefore at least \\(n-3\\) of the non‑sunny lines are\n   vertical.  Hence\n   \\[\n   n-k\\ge n-3\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le 3 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Assume a covering with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n   As in step 3, the columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) must be vertical.\n   The two remaining non‑sunny lines can be chosen only among the columns\n   \\(x=n-2,\\;x=n-1,\\;x=n\\).  \n   Those three columns contain respectively \\(3,2,1\\) points, i.e. six points in\n   total.  Two sunny lines can contribute at most one point to each of the three\n   columns, i.e. at most six points altogether, but a line can meet a column in\n   **exactly one** lattice point, so the two sunny lines together can cover at most\n   four of the six points.  Consequently at least two points remain uncovered – a\n   contradiction.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n6. **Existence for \\(k=0,1,3\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non‑sunny and\n   together contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   – For \\(n=3\\) use the three sunny lines\n     \\[\n     \\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n     \\ell_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad\n     \\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+5 ,\n     \\]\n     which cover the six points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\); the only sunny line is\n     \\(\\ell_1\\).  \n   – For \\(n\\ge4\\) take the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) (all non‑sunny) and\n     the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (non‑sunny).  The only uncovered point is\n     \\((n-1,2)\\).  The line through \\((n-1,2)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) has slope\n     \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-2\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it is sunny and together we have exactly\n     one sunny line.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   – For \\(n=3\\) the three sunny lines\n     \\[\n     \\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n     \\ell_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad\n     \\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n     \\]\n     cover all six points; they are the three sunny lines.\n\n   – For \\(n\\ge4\\) use the vertical lines\n     \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) (non‑sunny) and the three sunny lines\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\ell_1 &: \\; y-1 = -\\tfrac12\\,(x-n),          &&\\text{through }(n,1),(n-2,2),\\\\\n     \\ell_2 &: \\; y-1 = -2\\,(x-n+1),              &&\\text{through }(n-1,1),(n-2,3),\\\\\n     \\ell_3 &: \\; y-2 = 1\\,(x-n+1),                &&\\text{through }(n-1,2),(n-2,1).\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n     Their slopes are \\(-\\tfrac12,-2,1\\), none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence\n     they are sunny.  These three lines together cover the six points\n     \\((n,1),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3)\\), i.e. all points that are\n     not already covered by the vertical lines.  Thus we obtain a family of exactly\n     \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely three sunny ones.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Steps 4 and 5 show that no covering can have \\(k\\ge4\\) or \\(k=2\\); step 6 shows that\n   \\(k=0,1,3\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  Hence the set of admissible\n   numbers of sunny lines is exactly \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
