            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   *The required set of lattice points is  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Construction for any \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   *Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n   *Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of those vertical lines, so the covering condition is already satisfied.  \n   *Add \\(k\\) further lines, each passing through the point \\((1,1)\\) and having a distinct sunny slope (for instance slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\)).  \n   *All added lines are sunny, distinct from the vertical ones, and the covering condition remains true.  \n   Hence any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be realised.\n\n3. **A bound for a sunny line.**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line that meets the left side of the triangle \\(\\operatorname{conv}(S_n)\\) at the lattice point \\((1,b)\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)).  \n   Then the number of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(L\\) is at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-b}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\).  \n\n   *Proof of Lemma 1.*  \n   Because \\(L\\) is not parallel to the side \\(x+y=n+1\\), its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\neq-1\\).  \n   Write \\(m=\\frac{p}{q}\\) with coprime integers \\(q>0\\).  \n   Starting from \\((1,b)\\), the next lattice point on \\(L\\) is \\((1+q,b+p)\\); the next one is \\((1+2q,b+2p)\\), etc.  \n   All these points must satisfy the inequality \\(x+y\\le n+1\\).  \n   For the \\(t\\)-th point we have  \n   \\[\n   (1+tq)+(b+tp)=t(p+q)+(1+b)\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(p+q\\ge 2\\) (the cases \\(p+q=0\\) or \\(1\\) would give a slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), which are excluded), we obtain  \n   \\[\n   t\\le\\frac{n+1-(1+b)}{p+q}\\le\\frac{n-b}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Hence the number of lattice points on \\(L\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-b}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-b}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\), proving the lemma. ∎\n\n4. **Impossibility of having all \\(n\\) lines sunny when \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   *Lemma 2.*  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, the total number of incidences (pairs “point of \\(S_n\\) lying on a line”) is at most  \n   \\[\n   I_{\\max}=n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof of Lemma 2.*  \n   Every sunny line meets the side \\(x=1\\) of the triangle at a lattice point \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); otherwise the line would miss all points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   By Lemma 1 the line can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-b}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   The function \\(f(b)=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-b}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) is decreasing in \\(b\\); its maximum is attained at \\(b=1\\) and equals \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   Consequently each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) incidences, and with \\(n\\) lines we obtain (2). ∎\n\n   *Comparison with the required number of incidences.*  \n   From (1) the set \\(S_n\\) contains \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points, and each of them must lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines; therefore at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) incidences are needed.  \n   For \\(n\\ge4\\) we have\n   \\[\n   n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\;<\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Indeed,\n   * if \\(n\\) is even, \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=\\frac n2\\) and the left‑hand side of (3) equals \\(\\frac{n^{2}}2<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\);\n   * if \\(n\\) is odd, write \\(n=2m+1\\;(m\\ge1)\\). Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) and the left‑hand side of (3) is \\((2m+1)(m+1)=2m^{2}+3m+1\\), whereas the right‑hand side is \\((2m+1)(m+1)=2m^{2}+3m+1\\) **plus** the extra term \\(\\frac{2m+1}{2}\\). Hence the inequality is strict for every \\(m\\ge1\\).  \n\n   Because the maximal possible number of incidences is strictly smaller than the number required, it is impossible that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n5. **The exceptional case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n   The set \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) has three points.  \n   The three sunny lines  \n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad \n   L_2:\\;y=2x,\\qquad \n   L_3:\\;y=\\tfrac12x\n   \\]\n   pass respectively through \\((1,1)\\), \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n   They are distinct, none is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), and together they cover all points of \\(S_3\\).  \n   Hence for \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=n=3\\) is attainable.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   * For every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have exhibited an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   * Lemma 2 together with the co