by \\(n\\) sunny lines is impossible when\n\\(n\\ge4\\).  Consequently at least one line must be non‑sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1\\qquad (n\\ge4). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions\n\n#### 2.1  A covering with no sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B=\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\;y=1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies either \\(b=1\\) (hence lies on\n\\(y=1\\)) or \\(b\\ge2\\) (hence lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)).  All\n\\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realised for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Obtaining exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines, \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\).  \nStarting from \\(\\mathcal B\\), replace the vertical lines\n\\(x=n-t,\\;x=n-t+1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) by the sunny lines\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; \\text{through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(i+1,2)\\qquad(i=n-t,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(1\\) (hence sunny) and contains the two points\n\\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,2)\\).  The point \\((i+1,2)\\) was previously covered by\nthe vertical line \\(x=i+1\\), which is still present at the moment of the\nreplacement; therefore after the replacement all points of \\(S_n\\) remain\ncovered.  After performing the \\(t\\) replacements we have exactly \\(t\\) sunny\nlines (the \\(L_i\\)’s) and \\(n-t\\) non‑sunny lines (the remaining vertical\nlines together with \\(y=1\\)).  Hence every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) occurs.\n\n#### 2.3  The case \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nBegin with the configuration obtained for \\(t=n-2\\) in §2.2; it contains\n\\(n-2\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) and the two non‑sunny lines\n\\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  Replace the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) by\n\n\\[\nL^{\\ast}:\\; \\text{through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(1,2),\n\\qquad\\text{slope }-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n\\(L^{\\ast}\\) is sunny, covers the point \\((n,1)\\) (the only point of\n\\(S_n\\) not lying on