n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) plus the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).  \n  *Verification:* The added line has slope \\(-\\frac{1}{n-1}\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\); it is sunny. All points with \\(x=n\\) are covered by the sunny line (the only such point is \\((n,1)\\)). All other points are covered by the appropriate vertical line. Correct.\n\n---\n\n#### 5. Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) (exactly one non‑sunny line)\n\n##### 5.1 Definition of \\(\\mathcal A_i\\) (6)\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_i=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;\n\\frac{b-1}{a-i}\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\n- The intention is that any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal A_i\\) yields a sunny line through \\((i,1)\\) and \\((a,b)\\).  \n  *Correct* – the three forbidden slopes correspond exactly to horizontal, slope \\(-1\\), and vertical.\n\n##### 5.2 Claim “\\(|\\mathcal A_i|\\ge n-2\\) for every \\(i\\ge2\\)”\n\n- **Counter‑example:** Take \\(n=4\\) and \\(i=4\\). The interior points of \\(S_4\\) are \\((2,2),(2,3),(3,2)\\). Only \\((2,2)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal A_4\\); thus \\(|\\mathcal A_4|=1\\), while \\(n-2=2\\).  \n  Hence the inequality is false. This is a **Critical Error** (factual mistake).\n\n##### 5.3 Claim “each \\(\\mathcal A_i\\) contains at least one point whose first coordinate equals \\(i\\) (namely \\((i,2)\\) … and for \\(i=n\\) the point \\((n,2)\\) also belongs to \\(\\mathcal A_n\\)).”\n\n- For \\(i=n\\) the point \\((n,2)\\) does **not** satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) (it gives \\(n+2>n+1\\)). Moreover, for any \\(i\\) the point \\((i,2)\\) is not in \\(S_n\\) when \\(i=n\\).  \n  Hence this statement is false; another **Critical Error**.\n\n##### 5.4 Hall’s marriage argument\n\n- The proof of Hall’s condition relies on the (incorrect) claim that each \\(\\mathcal A_i\\) contains a point with first coordinate \\(i\\). Without that, the inequality \\(|\\bigcup_{i\\in U}\\mathcal A_i|\\ge|U|\\) is not established.  \n  Therefore the application of Hall’s theorem is **unjustified** – a **Justification Gap**.\n\n##### 5.5 Distinctness of the lines \\(L_i\\)\n\n- The solution asserts that different second points guarantee different slopes. This is false: e.g. points \\((2,3)\\) and \\((3,4)\\) both give slope \\(1\\) when paired with \\((1,1)\\). The argument does not rule out coincident slopes, so the claim that the lines are distinct is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n##### 5.6 Covering property\n\n- Assuming the matching exists, the description of the covering is correct: vertical line \\(x=1\\) covers all left‑side points, each \\(L_i\\) covers its bottom point and the matched interior point. However, because the existence of the matching and the distinctness of the lines have not been proved, the covering claim remains on shaky ground – another **Justification Gap**.\n\n---\n\n#### 6. “Intermediate values of \\(k\\)” – replacement argument\n\n- The argument replaces a sunny line \\(L_i\\) by the vertical line \\(x=i\\) and claims that the interior point \\(P_i\\) remains covered because it has the same first coordinate \\(i\\).  \n- As shown in 5.3, the construction does **not** guarantee that \\(P_i\\) has first coordinate \\(i\\); indeed for \\(i=n\\) no such interior point exists. Consequently the replacement may leave some interior points uncovered.  \n- This is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n---\n\n#### 7. Case \\(n=3\\)\n\n- The enumeration of the six points of \\(S_3\\) is correct.  \n- The three explicit configurations for \\(k=0,1,3\\) are valid.  \n- The impossibility of \\(k=2\\) is argued by “the remaining line must be either vertical or horizontal; such a line can cover at most one point of the bottom side…”.  \n  *Verification:* With two sunny lines, the third line must be non‑sunny. A non‑sunny line can be vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\). Any such line can indeed contain at most one point of the bottom side \\(\\{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\). Since the two sunny lines each already contain one bottom point (other