ndition holds, and there exists a **matching**\n     \\[\n     i\\longmapsto P_i\\in\\mathcal A_i\\qquad(i=2,\\dots ,n)\n     \\tag{8}\n     \\]\n     that assigns distinct interior points to distinct bottom points.\n\n   - Define \\(L_i\\) as the line through \\((i,1)\\) and \\(P_i\\) \\((i=2,\\dots ,n)\\).  \n     By construction each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, the lines are distinct (they have different slopes because the second point is different), and together with the vertical line \\(x=1\\) they cover every point of \\(S_n\\):\n       * the vertical line covers all points of \\(L_n\\);\n       * each bottom point \\((i,1)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\);\n       * each interior point belongs to the unique line that was matched to it;\n       * the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n   Thus we have a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n   *Intermediate values of \\(k\\).*  \n   Starting from the configuration described above (which has \\(n-1\\) sunny lines), replace arbitrarily any subset of the sunny lines by the vertical line that has the same \\(x\\)-coordinate as the bottom point of the replaced line.  \n   A vertical line is non‑sunny, it still contains the bottom point \\((i,1)\\) and also contains the interior point \\(P_i\\) (because \\(P_i\\) has the same first coordinate \\(i\\)).  \n   Consequently the covering property is preserved while the number of sunny lines is reduced by exactly the number of replacements.  \n   By performing \\(r\\) replacements we obtain a configuration with \\(k=(n-1)-r\\) sunny lines, where \\(0\\le r\\le n-1\\).  \n   Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is realizable for \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n4. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n   A direct inspection shows:\n   * \\(k=0\\) – three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\);\n   * \\(k=1\\) – two vertical lines \\(x=1,2\\) and the su