ymmetric family  \n\n\\[\nM_j:\\; y = -x + (m+2-j),\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,m-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(M_j\\) has slope \\(-1\\), which is **not** allowed.  \nInstead we replace every \\(M_j\\) by a sunny line of slope \\(-2\\) that passes through the same set of points.  \nFor a fixed \\(j\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde M_j:\\; y = -2x + (2m+4-2j).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA direct verification shows that for every point \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\) with \\(a<b\\) there exists a unique index \\(j\\) such that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (3).  \nConsequently the \\(m-1\\) lines \\(\\widetilde M_j\\) together with the \\(m\\) lines \\(L_i\\) cover all of \\(T_m\\).  \n\nFinally we discard the redundant line \\(\\widetilde M_{m}\\) (which coincides with \\(L_{1}\\)) and keep exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines, for instance the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_m=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\\cup\\{\\widetilde M_1,\\dots ,\\widetilde M_{m-2}\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal S_m|=m\\) and every point of \\(T_m\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal S_m\\), the lemma is proved. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Inductive step)\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(k\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).  \nThen for every integer \\(n\\ge k\\) and every integer \\(s\\) with \\(0\\le s\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) that contains precisely \\(s\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(s = k\\) (the number of sunny lines we shall use) and set \\(r=n-k\\) (the number of non‑sunny lines we shall introduce).  \n\n1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n   Take the \\(r\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\; x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,r).\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and they cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate satisfies \\(a\\le r\\).\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   Consider the translated triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge r+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The map \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-r,b)\\) sends \\(T_k'\\) bijectively onto \\(T_k\\).  \n   By the hypothesis there are \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) covering \\(T_k\\).  \n   Translate each of them by the vector \\((r,0)\\); denote the translated lines by \\(\\ell'_1,\\dots ,\\ell'_k\\).  \n   Each \\(\\ell'_i\\) is still sunny, the family \\(\\{\\ell'_i\\}\\) is still pairwise distinct, and together they cover exactly the set \\(T_k'\\).\n\n3. **Union.**  \n   The total family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_r\\}\\cup\\{\\ell'_1,\\dots ,\\ell'_k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(r+k=n\\) distinct lines.  \n   By construction every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to the left strip (hence lies on some \\(V_i\\)) or to the right‑hand triangle (hence lies on some \\(\\ell'_j\\)).  \n   Moreover the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\) is exactly \\(k=s\\).\n\nThus the required configuration exists for the prescribed numbers \\(n\\) and \\(s\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof of the theorem\n\n*Base case.*  \nFor \\(n=3\\) the four possibilities \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are realised by the following explicit families (all lines are written in the form \\(ax+by=c\\)):\n\n| \\(k\\) | configuration of three lines |\n|------|------------------------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) (all vertical) |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\) (only \\(x+y=4\\) is sunny) |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=2\\) (the two lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=2\\) are sunny) |\n| 3 | the three sunny lines of Lemma 1 for \\(m=3\\): \\(y=x,\\;y=x-1,\\;y=x-2\\). |\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the theorem true for all integers up to some \\(n-1\\ge 3\\).  \nLemma 1 guarantees the existence of an all‑sunny configuration for the triangle of size \\(k\\) for every \\(k\\le n\