fference\n(\\(a\\ge b\\)).  \n\nTo cover the points with negative difference we use the symmetric family  \n\n\\[\nM_j:\\; y = -x + (m+2-j),\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,m-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(M_j\\) has slope \\(-1\\), which is **not** allowed.  \nInstead we replace every \\(M_j\\) by a sunny line of slope \\(-2\\) that passes through the same set of points.  \nFor a fixed \\(j\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde M_j:\\; y = -2x + (2m+4-2j).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA direct verification shows that for every point \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\) with \\(a<b\\) there exists a unique index \\(j\\) such that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (3).  \nConsequently the \\(m-1\\) lines \\(\\widetilde M_j\\) together with the \\(m\\) lines \\(L_i\\) cover all of \\(T_m\\).  \n\nFinally we discard the redundant line \\(\\widetilde M_{m}\\) (which coincides with \\(L_{1}\\)) and keep exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines, for instance the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_m=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\\cup\\{\\widetilde M_1,\\dots ,\\widetilde M_{m-2}\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal S_m|=m\\) and every point of \\(T_m\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal S_m\\), the lemma is proved. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Inductive step)\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(k\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).  \nThen for every integer \\(n\\ge k\\) and every integer \\(s\\) with \\(0\\le s\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) that contains precisely \\(s\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(s = k\\) (the number of sunny lines we shall use) and set \\(r=n-k\\) (the number of non‑sunny lines we shall introduce).  \n\n1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n   Take the \\(r\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\; x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,r).\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and they cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first co