lope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n  \\(L'\\) is distinct from all previously used lines, is sunny, and together with the original \\(n\\) lines we obtain a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k=n+1\\) sunny lines. By (2) every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\nThus, starting from a configuration for \\(n\\) we have produced configurations for \\(n+1\\) with every admissible value of \\(k\\). Hence \\(P(n+1)\\) holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBy induction, \\(P(n)\\) is true for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\). Consequently, for each \\(n\\ge3\\) and each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition (1) together with the constructive existence proved above shows that the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
