-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have found a complete solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch** –  \n1. **Construction for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).**  \n   * \\(k=0\\): use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   * \\(k=1\\): use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and one sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).  \n2. **Upper bounds for interior points.**  \n   * For the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (vertical or horizontal) the maximum number of interior points they can cover is  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{(n-k)(\\,n+k-1\\,)}{2}.\n     \\]\n   * For the \\(k\\) sunny lines the maximum number of interior points they can cover is  \n     \\[\n     \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(k-1)^2}{4}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n     \\]\n3. **Contradiction for \\(k\\ge2\\).**  \n   The total number of interior points in the triangle \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) is \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\).  \n   For \\(k\\ge2\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-k)(\\,n+k-1\\,)}{2}+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(k-1)^2}{4}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\;<\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}{2},\n   \\]\n   which shows that the \\(n\\) lines cannot cover all interior points.  \n   Hence \\(k\\ge2\\) is impossible.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nThe outer diagonal of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\nD_n=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(n\\) points.  \nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) are interior points; their