                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nThe points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on the outer diagonal; the remaining\npoints (the **interior points**) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \nTheir number is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b\\le n\\}\\,|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}(n-a)=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. its slope is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the\ncovering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nLet the other \\(n-k\\) lines be called **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Non‑sunny lines.**  \nA non‑sunny line is either vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or\nhas slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).  \nIf a vertical line is \\(x=i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) then the interior points on\nit are \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-i\\); there are \\(n-i\\) of them.\nThe same number is obtained for a horizontal line \\(y=i\\), and a line\n\\(x+y=s\\;(2\\le s\\le n)\\) contains \\(s-1\\le n-1\\) interior points.\nHence among all non‑sunny lines the vertical ones give the largest\npossible contribution.\n\nConsequently the total number of interior points that can be covered by\nthe \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines is maximised by taking the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k-1\n\\]\n\ntogether with the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (which contains no interior point).\nThe number of interior points covered by these lines equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{\\,n-k-1}(n-i)=\\sum_{j=k+1}^{\\,n-1}j\n   =\\frac{n(n-1)}2-\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus after the non‑sunny lines are fixed there remain exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ninterior points that must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Sunny lines.**  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Because it is not parallel to any side of\nthe triangle, \\(L\\) meets the triangle in two distinct points, one on a\nside \\(x=1\\) and the other on a side \\(y=1\\) (the case where an endpoint\nlies on the outer diagonal is analogous).  Write these intersection\npoints as  \n\n\\[\nP=(1,p),\\qquad Q=(q,1),\\qquad 1\\le p,q\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThe vector \\(\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\) is \\((q-1,\\,1-p)\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\nd=\\gcd(q-1,\\;p-1).\n\\]\n\nAll lattice points on the segment \\(PQ\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(1+t\\frac{q-1}{d},\\;p-t\\frac{p-1}{d}\\bigr),\\qquad t=0,1,\\dots ,d .\n\\]\n\nThe two endpoints lie on the boundary, so the number of **interior**\nlattice points on \\(L\\) is at most \\(d\\) (it is \\(d-1\\) when both\nendpoints are on the boundary, and \\(d\\) when one of them is the interior\npoint \\((1,1)\\)).  Because \\(L\\) is sunny we have \\(q-1\\neq p-1\\); otherwise\nthe slope would be \\(-1\\).  Hence \\(q-1\\) and \\(p-1\\) are distinct positive\nintegers not exceeding \\(n-1\\).  The largest possible value of their\ngreatest common divisor is\n\n\\[\n\\max\\{\\gcd(u,v):1\\le u<v\\le n-1\\}\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore every sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ninterior points.\n\nMoreover, the bound in (5) can be attained only when\n\\(\\{q-1,p-1\\}=\\{d,2d\\}\\) with \\(d=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\);\nthere is only **one** unordered pair with this property, so at most one\nsunny line can achieve the maximum.  By decreasing one of the two\ncoordinates we obtain a sunny line with exactly one fewer interior point,\nand repeating this