  For each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  d_j=\n  \\begin{cases}\n     n-2j      &\\text{if }n\\equiv j\\pmod2,\\\\[2pt]\n     n-2j+1    &\\text{otherwise},\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\qquad\\text{(so that }d_j\\equiv n+1\\pmod2\\text{ and }0\\le d_j\\le n-1\\text{).}\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\n  The line  \n\n  \\[\n  S_j:\\;y=x-d_j\n  \\tag{10}\n  \\]\n\n  has slope \\(1\\); therefore it is sunny.  Its intersection with the\n  bottom side \\(y=1\\) is \\((d_j+1,1)\\) and with the outer diagonal\n  \\(x+y=n+1\\) is \\(\\bigl(\\frac{n+1+d_j}2,\\frac{n+1-d_j}2\\bigr)\\), an\n  integer point because of the parity condition in (9).\n\n  A direct check shows that the interior lattice points on \\(S_j\\) are\n\n  \\[\n  (d_j+1,1),\\;(d_j+2,2),\\dots ,(d_j+j,j),\n  \\tag{11}\n  \\]\n\n  i.e. exactly \\(j\\) points.  Moreover these points are precisely the\n  points of \\(T_k\\) whose abscissa equals \\(n-k+j-1\\).  Hence the\n  \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) together cover all points of \\(T_k\\).\n\nThe total number of lines used is  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(L_0)+ (n-k-1)\\;(L_i)+k\\;(S_j)=n,\n\\]\n\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at\nleast one of them.  This construction works for every admissible\n\\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Answer\n\nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\,}.\n\\]"
