                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in \\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(S\\) are exactly the lattice points that have to lie on the\n\\(n\\) lines.  \nWrite  \n\n* \\(D=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\) – the **outer diagonal** ( \\(|D|=n\\) ),\n* \\(I=S\\setminus D\\) – the **interior points** ( \\(|I|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) ).\n\nA line is called **sunny** when its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nAssume  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that\ncover all points of \\(S\\).\n\n* **(i)  A non‑sunny line for the outer diagonal.**  \n  The line  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_{0}:\\;x+y=n+1\n  \\]\n\n  has slope \\(-1\\); it contains every point of \\(D\\).\n\n* **(ii)  Non‑sunny vertical lines.**  \n  For  \n\n  \\[\n  t=2,3,\\dots ,n-k\n  \\]\n\n  let  \n\n  \\[\n  v_{t}:\\;x=t .\n  \\]\n\n  These lines are vertical, hence non‑sunny, and they are distinct from\n  \\(\\ell_{0}\\).\n\n* **(iii)  Sunny lines.**  \n  For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  P_{i}=(n-i,\\;i+1)\\in D ,\\qquad\n  Q_{i}=(1,\\;n-i)\\in\\{x=1\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Let  \n\n  \\[\n  s_{i}:\\text{ the line through }P_{i}\\text{ and }Q_{i}.\n  \\]\n\n  The slope of \\(s_{i}\\) is  \n\n  \\[\n  m_{i}= \\frac{(i+1)-(n-i)}{(n-i)-1}\n        =\\frac{2i+1-n}{\\,n-i-1\\,}.\n  \\]\n\n  Because \\(i\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) we have\n  \\(2i+1\\neq n\\); consequently \\(m_{i}\\neq -1,0,\\infty\\).  Hence each\n  \\(s_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\n  **Lattice points on \\(s_{i}\\).**  \n  The vector  \n\n  \\[\n  \\overrightarrow{Q_{i}P_{i}}=(n-i-1,\\;-(n-2i-1))\n  \\]\n\n  satisfies  \n\n  \\[\n  \\gcd\\bigl(n-i-1,\\;n-2i-1\\bigr)=\\gcd\\bigl(n-