\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\).\n  The integer points on \\(L_i\\) are  \n\n  \\[\n  (2i-n+t,\\;1+t)\\qquad t=0,1,\\dots ,n-i .\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\n  The point with \\(t=0\\) is \\((2i-n,1)\\) (already counted on the\n  bottom side of the triangle), the point with \\(t=n-i\\) is the outer‑\n  diagonal point \\((i,n+1-i)\\); the intermediate \\(n-i-1\\) points are\n  interior points.  Hence each line \\(L_i\\) contributes exactly  \n\n  \\[\n  n-i\\quad\\text{interior points}.\n  \\tag{10}\n  \\]\n\n  Summing (10) for the \\(k\\) values of \\(i\\) in (7) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{i=n-k}^{\\,n-1}(n-i)=\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}.\n  \\tag{11}\n  \\]\n\n* **(iv)  Counting all interior points.**  \n  Adding the contributions (6) and (11) we obtain  \n\n  \\[\n  \\frac{(n-k-1)(\\,n+k-2\\,)}{2}+\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\n  =\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\n  =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}-(n-1).\n  \\tag{12}\n  \\]\n\n  The missing \\((n-1)\\) interior points are precisely the points on the\n  bottom side \\(y=1\\) with abscissae  \n\n  \\[\n  1,2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n  \\]\n\n  They are covered as follows:\n\n  * the points \\((c,1)\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-k-1\\) lie on the vertical lines\n    \\(V_c\\);\n  * the points \\((2i-n,1)\\) with \\(i=n-k,\\dots ,n-1\\) lie on the sunny\n    lines \\(L_i\\);\n  * the point \\((n,1)\\) lies on the outer diagonal \\(L_0\\).\n\n  Consequently **every** point of \\(T_n\\) is contained in at least one of the\n  \\(n\\) constructed lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  This proves\n  that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2) is admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the two conditions,\nand let exactly \\(k\\) of them be sunny.\n\n* **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n  A non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\).\n  Among these three families the vertical lines contain the largest possible