r\\rfloor .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nUsing (15) and the elementary inequality  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-k-1)(\\,n+k-2\\,)}{2}+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\;<\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}{2},\n\\qquad(k>\\tfrac{n-1}{2}),\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nwe see that the total number of interior points that can be covered is\nstrictly smaller than the required number \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) given by\n(1).  Hence such a configuration cannot exist.  The inequality (17) is\nobtained by expanding the left‑hand side, cancelling the term\n\\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\), and observing that the remaining expression equals\n\\(-\\bigl(k-\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\bigl(k-\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor+1\\bigr)\\le0\\).\n\nTherefore **no** configuration with more than\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines can satisfy the\nrequirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown\n\n* for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n  a concrete construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing all points of\n  \\(T_n\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines;\n* that no configuration can exist with a larger number of sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\,}.\n\\]"
